## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of on-device, privacy-preserving ML systems with per-domain adapters that load dynamically while maintaining strict privacy guarantees in a chat application context.

## Key Concepts
- On-device fine-tuning with per-domain adapters
- Dynamic adapter loading from secure knowledge bases
- Adapter injection points (attention vs FFN layers)
- Quantization trade-offs (4-bit vs 8-bit)
- Domain gating and routing strategies
- Memory budgeting for mobile deployment
- Cross-domain evaluation and privacy compliance

## Code Example
```javascript
// Adapter hub loads domain-specific adapters dynamically
class AdapterHub {
  async loadAdapter(domain) {
    const adapter = await this.secureKB.fetch(domain);
    this.activeAdapters.set(domain, adapter);
  }

  route(query) {
    return this.gatingModel.predict(query);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes how a candidate designs an actionable, beginner-friendly eval for grounding-heavy QA in a cross-brand setting (Tesla, MongoDB). It tests practicality, grounding fidelity, and the complete flow from data to metrics—not just theoretical understanding.

## Key Concepts

- **Grounding to versioned KB with provenance**: Each claim must reference a specific KB paragraph ID and version, enabling traceability
- **Hallucination taxonomy tailored for product support**: Fabrication (invented facts), misattribution (wrong brand/context), over-generalization (unverified sweeping claims)
- **Relevance to user intent**: Measuring whether responses actually address what users asked
- **Lightweight evaluation scripts**: Minimal, runnable code that beginners can extend

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple grounding verifier sketch
function groundClaim(claim, kb) {
  for (const p of kb.paragraphs) {
    if (claim.includes(p.key)) {
      return { grounded: true, paraId: p.id, version: p.version };
    }
  }
  return { grounded: false };
}

// Hallucination classifier
function classifyHallucination(claim, brand) {
  if (isInvented(claim)) return 'fabrication';
  if (wrongBrand(claim, brand)) return 'misattribution';
  if (isSweeping(claim)) return 'over-generalization';
  return 'valid';
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Understanding cross-language adapter strategies with strict latency and memory constraints.

## Key Concepts
- PEFT vs full fine-tuning in multi-language QA
- Adapter injection points (attention vs MLP)
- Language tagging vs per-language adapters
- Latency/memory budgeting and rollback plans

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo adapter injection sketch
function attachAdapters(model, keys) {
  for (const m of model.modules()) {
    if (m instanceof AttentionLayer || m instanceof MLP) {
      m.addAdapter({rank: 8, name: keys.next()});
    }
  }
  return model;
}
```

